1|0@CasualConversation^1440363721#cud651o:nedos$:No I meant contract. It's the same in Canada, except we don't pay for our lines. At least not in Montreal. You just have your phone plan that you pay for, and that's it. Also, all companies in the US seem to offer internet while in Canada, a plan without internet are still a thing. I always went for those because they were cheaper. My last phone plan was 30 CAD I believe, which is 23 USD. Unlimited text, limited calls and no internet, which was good for me because I barely use my cellphone. The cheapest I found in the US (out of the big cellphone providers) was 45 with AT&amp;T! Eek! 
1|0@CasualConversation^1440364973#cud6ux5:puttysan$:What is the difference between contract and plan? Aren't they the same thing? 
1|0@CasualConversation^1440366300#cud7l6c:nedos$:Well my previous provider did not have contracts at all. You could literally leave whenever you wanted. But they had plans you had to pay at the end of each month you were with them, or at the beginning of them (if you went for a prepaid plan). Their strategy though was to give out phones for very cheap, or for "free", and you had to pay them each month with a tab: small (5CAD), medium (10CAD), and big (15CAD). If I recall correctly. So with a small tab, you were stuck with them for at least two years, despite not having a contract. But of course, you can choose to pay the phone fully and only pay every month, and leave whenever you want to. 
2|0@CasualConversation^1440376134#cudd7u8:lupusdude$:[Republic Wireless](https://republicwireless.com/). My total payment after taxes and fees (and a refund for unused data) last month was less than $20 for a 0.5 GB plan. The catch: * Their cellular service piggybacks on Sprint, which doesn't have quite as extensive coverage as other services. (This is somewhat mitigated by free roaming.) * They keep the monthly bills so cheap by encouraging you to use WiFi as much as you can. That means phone calls are transmitted over WiFi when possible, with seamless transitioning to cellular when you move out of WiFi range. The upside is that using WiFi so much minimizes your cellular data usage (and is why I only need a half gig of data per month). * Your choice of phones is limited to the Moto E and Moto X, though they are very good phones if you like Android. * The phones are not subsidized, so the initial cost is high compared to other services. If you can live with all that, Republic is a good service if you want to keep your monthly costs down.
